Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.360
okay let's do some calculations
regarding the molar mass of a gas. if we

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:16.440
have a one point three one gram sample
of a hydrocarbon we vaporize it and it

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:22.830
is found to occupy two point zero liters
at 25 degrees Celsius and one point zero

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:28.830
atmospheres what is the gas we want to
identify this gas now we're gonna

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:33.180
probably need to use some information
regarding ideal gases to answer this

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.260
question so if you need to review ideal
gases go ahead and check out that

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.980
tutorial and when you're ready give this a try.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.359
so we have our given information one

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:06.240
point three one grams 2.0 liters 25
degrees Celsius and 1 atmosphere so

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.320
we're definitely going to need to use
the ideal gas law in order to answer

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.490
this question
so that's PV equals nRT and if we want

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:19.320
to identify that gas we're probably
going to need the molar mass and molar

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.900
mass is grams per mole we have grams but
we don't have moles so let's solve for

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.939
it let's solve for n in the ideal gas
law so n is going to be equal to PV over

00:01:29.939 --> 00:01:35.820
RT so now let's just plug in what we
know n or the number of moles in this

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.070
sample of gas are going to be equal to
one point zero zero atmospheres times

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:46.680
two point zero liters that's the volume
of the sample divided by the gas

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.320
constant R which in this case we're
going to use the version that is 0.0821

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.259
liters atmospheres per mole Kelvin
because we're going to want to cancel

00:01:55.259 --> 00:02:00.000
out those other units and then the
temperature is 25 degrees Celsius but

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.930
remember we always want to express that
in Kelvin when we're doing calculations

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:09.360
so that is going to be 298 Kelvin and
then if we plug all that into the

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:16.390
calculator we get 0.08 to
so that is how many moles are in this

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:21.130
sample so now if we know the mass of
this sample of gas and we know how many

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:26.710
moles it represents all we have to do is
take the value in grams and divide by

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:33.790
the value in moles and we'll get grams
per mole so 1.31 grams over 0.08 two

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:40.150
moles is approximately 16 grams per mole
and now while this could be a few

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:45.070
different things we know it is a
hydrocarbon and so the only hydrocarbon

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:48.550
that is gonna have this molar mass is
gonna be methane because we need at

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:52.420
least one carbon atom that's 12 and then
we have no more room for a second one

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.950
and just add four hydrogen atoms on
there and we've gotten methane so this

00:02:56.950 --> 00:03:00.040
gas must have been methane.

